<h2>Who we work for</h2>
<p>All Sewer and Plumbing Services takes on residential, commercial, industrial and municipal work from our base in Westminster. The methods are the same ones we use on houses, <a href="/sewer-camera-inspection/">camera inspection</a>, <a href="/hydro-jetting/">hydro jetting</a>, <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">Perma-Liner CIPP lining</a> and <a href="/pipe-bursting/">fused HDPE pipe bursting</a>, scaled to larger pipe and planned around the people who use the building.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Apartment and condominium buildings</strong>, where one shared line can serve dozens of units</li>
  <li><strong>HOA communities and townhome complexes</strong>, with private sewer lines running under common landscaping and drives</li>
  <li><strong>Retail centers, offices and restaurants</strong>, where a dig through a parking lot or storefront costs business every day it is open</li>
  <li><strong>Industrial and warehouse property</strong>, often with larger lines under heavily loaded pavement</li>
  <li><strong>Schools, public facilities and municipalities</strong>, including storm and specialty drains</li>
</ul>

<h2>More than sanitary sewers</h2>
<p>Lining is not limited to the line that carries wastewater to the main. We have also lined pool plumbing, storm drains and roof drains, and we work on commercial kitchen grease lines. One of our larger jobs was lining 200 feet of 12-inch drain line serving a university pool in Denver, which avoided excavating around the facility.</p>
<p>Roof drains that run down through a building and out under the slab are a good example of where trenchless pays off. Opening them conventionally means cutting floors inside an occupied space. Lining from the access points at either end leaves the building alone.</p>

<h2>Survey first, then a plan</h2>
<p>Commercial scopes start with a full video survey of the lines in question. We map where each line runs, how deep it is, what it is made of and where the defects are. On a multi-building property, that survey becomes the basis for a prioritized plan: what needs attention now, what can wait, and what only needs maintenance.</p>
<p>Many commercial and multifamily properties in the metro were built in the same decades as the surrounding subdivisions and share the same weak points. Clay and cast iron lines in expansive soil develop offsets and sags, and parking lot trees, often cottonwood, silver maple or Siberian elm, send roots into any joint they can reach. Knowing which lines are failing, and why, keeps a board or owner from paying to fix everything at once.</p>

<h2>Working around occupants</h2>
<p>A lining or bursting job on an occupied building is as much about scheduling as it is about pipe. We plan work in phases by building, line or stack so that no one loses service longer than necessary, give residents or tenants specific shut-off windows, and schedule restaurant and retail work around business hours when that is what it takes. Lining often goes in from existing cleanouts, which means no one's unit or storefront gets opened.</p>

<h2>Grease lines</h2>
<p>Kitchen lines clog with grease no matter how good the trap maintenance is. Routine <a href="/hydro-jetting/">jetting</a> handles the buildup. Over years, grease and cleaning chemicals can also eat at older pipe until it leaks or breaks, at which point the line needs structural repair. We handle both sides and can set up a maintenance schedule so backups do not arrive during a dinner rush.</p>

<h2>When lining or bursting will not work</h2>
<p>The limits that apply to a house apply to a commercial line too. A line that has lost grade, carries long bellies, or has collapsed in places may need excavation or a <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">spot repair</a>. Bursting needs room for displaced pipe, which can rule it out near foundations or crowded utility corridors. We say so in the scope and design the job around it, often combining a small excavation with lining on the rest of the run.</p>

<h2>Districts, permits and ownership</h2>
<p>Commercial properties answer to the same patchwork of city utilities and special sanitation districts that residential ones do, and larger jobs often draw closer review. Some districts restrict certain lining methods or require specific inspection steps. We confirm requirements with the city or district before we schedule. In most Front Range cities and districts, the private service line is the property owner's responsibility all the way to the public main, including the tap and any portion under the street, so we plan for right-of-way work where the survey shows it is needed.</p>

<h2>Where we work</h2>
<p>We serve commercial clients throughout the Denver metro and Front Range, from <a href="/denver/">Denver</a> and <a href="/adams-county/">Adams County</a> to <a href="/arapahoe-county/aurora/">Aurora</a>, <a href="/jefferson-county/lakewood/">Lakewood</a>, <a href="/boulder-county/boulder/">Boulder</a>, <a href="/larimer-county/fort-collins/">Fort Collins</a> and <a href="/weld-county/greeley/">Greeley</a>. The full list is on our <a href="/service-areas/">service areas page</a>. We are open 24 hours a day, seven days a week, and see <a href="/our-process/">our process</a> for how a project moves from survey to final video.</p>
